    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Although several criteria have been proposed regarding the conditions for an opening-mode fracture to propagate across a pre-existing interface, the problem has not been solved completely: all of the existing theories deal only with the special case where the propagating fracture is perpendicular to the interface. In nature, however, the acute angle between a propagating fracture and the interface it is approaching may be arbitrary, ranging from 0° to 90°. This more general case has been studied analytically in this paper by considering the stress field around the fracture tip, the status of the pre-existing interface as controlled by this stress field, and the estimated dimension of the process zone. The results show that the transition between crossing and not crossing depends on the remote principal stress ratio, the angle between the propagating fracture and the interface, the coefficient of friction along the interface, and the tensile strength of the rock. Under a certain stress condition, crossing occurs more easily when the angle between the propagating fracture and the interface is high. Also, a greater coefficient of friction along the interface is favorable for crossing. Furthermore, re-initiation of fracture occurs on the opposite side of the interface where the angle between the interface and the propagating fracture is acute. The step length (i.e., the distance between the propagating fracture and the fracture initiated on the opposite side of the interface) depends on the size of the process zone, which is a function of the fracture toughness, tensile strength and the remote tensile stress acting normal to the fracture.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
